{"entries":{"chapter-79-study-hypertension|Chapter 79 Study: Hypertension":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Arterial stiffness drives age-associated systolic and pulse-pressure elevation; reduced baroreflex sensitivity increases variability and postural/postprandial hypotension (p1219, p1221).":["u3"],"copy|Renal sodium retention, sympathetic activity, central adiposity and inactivity contribute. High blood pressure should not be dismissed as normal aging (p1219, p1221).":["u4"],"copy|Measure after at least 5 minutes seated rest with feet on the floor, appropriate cuff and standardized technique (p1222).":["u5"],"copy|Diagnose from repeated averages, not a single value; the chapter describes multiple readings over separate visits or 24-hour ambulatory/home measurements (p1219, p1222).":["u6"],"copy|The chapter's cited guideline lowered the systolic diagnostic threshold to 130 mm Hg (p1220).":["u7"],"copy|Assess renal disease/proteinuria, retinal and cardiac effects, cardiovascular risks, frailty and comorbidity (p1223).":["u8"],"copy|For ambulatory community-living older adults, the cited guideline uses a systolic goal of 130 mm Hg; high comorbidity burden or limited life expectancy calls for patient-informed judgment (p1223).":["u9"],"copy|SPRINT compared systolic targets below 120 versus 140 mm Hg. In participants at least 75, intensive treatment reduced the primary cardiovascular outcome by 34% and all-cause mortality by 33% (p1223).":["u10"],"copy|Support sodium reduction, weight management and physical activity; lifestyle changes remain useful alongside medication (p1224).":["u11"],"copy|Select medication using comorbid indications, tolerability, cost and regimen simplicity; achieved BP reduction matters more than class alone (p1224–1225).":["u12"],"copy|Thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors/ARBs and calcium-channel antagonists are options; ACE inhibitors/ARBs are often used with diabetes or heart failure (p1225).":["u13"],"copy|Beta blockers are not initial therapy solely for hypertension in older adults without a compelling indication (p1225).":["u14"],"copy|Monitor supine and upright pressure: treatment-related hypotension may present as weakness or fatigue rather than classic dizziness (p1223).":["u15"],"copy|If seated systolic pressure cannot fall below 130 mm Hg without postural hypotension, consider a standing-pressure-based target (p1223).":["u16"],"copy|SPRINT's dementia reduction was not statistically significant, although mild cognitive impairment was reduced; do not describe definite dementia prevention (p1223).":["u17"],"copy|Individual treatment goals still matter even when trials demonstrate benefit in community-dwelling frail subgroups (p1223).":["u18"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u19"],"copy|2025-Jun Q53":["u20"],"copy|CHAPTER 79s / HAZZARD":["u21"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 79 - Hypertension; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u22"],"copy|Mechanisms & risk":["u23"],"copy|Diagnosis":["u24"],"copy|Targets & management":["u25"],"copy|Drugs & cautions":["u26"],"copy|Exam traps":["u27"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u28"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (6):":["u28"],"copy|For ambulatory community-living older adults, p1223 summarizes the goal as 130 mm Hg. The 2017 guideline specifies below 130 if average systolic pressure is at least 130. Substantial comorbidity or limited life expectancy calls for individualized judgment.":["u9"],"copy|For ambulatory community-living older adults, p1223 summarizes the goal as 130 mm Hg. Pages 1220 and 1225, and the 2017 guideline, specify below 130 if average systolic pressure is at least 130. Substantial comorbidity or limited life expectancy calls for individualized judgment.":["u9"],"copy|At 165/90 after months of lifestyle changes, a 75-year-old will usually need two agents from the outset, according to the chapter.":["u58"],"copy|The prose on p1223 summarizes the goal as 130 mm Hg; pp1220 and 1225 specify below 130.":["u73"],"copy|Progressive proximal weakness, bruising or broad purple striae can justify testing for hypercortisolism.":["u88"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Use plasma free or urinary fractionated metanephrines for suspected catecholamine excess.":["u177"],"copy|Overnight dexamethasone suppression is one initial test for suspected hypercortisolism.":["u178"],"copy|Monitor for hypotension, renal injury and electrolyte disturbance (pp1222–1223).":["u106"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Nursing-home residents and people with dementia, diabetes, prior stroke, standing systolic pressure below 110 or a life expectancy under three years were excluded.":["u107"],"copy|A patient with advanced dependency and recurrent aspiration needs a discussion of prognosis, burdens and goals.":["u108"],"copy|Hazzard reserves beta blockers for compelling indications, specifically secondary prevention after MI or in coronary artery disease.":["u179"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Long-term beta blockade for a prior MI may be reassessed after one year in patients without reduced ventricular function, angina, arrhythmia or uncontrolled hypertension.":["u180"],"copy|CHAPTER 79 / HAZZARD":["u21"]},"maxId":180,"sequenceOffsets":[[181,-152],[210,-151],[224,-150],[237,-148],[254,-144],[264,-142],[319,0]]}
